    45	\noindent {\bf Abstract:} We consider the existence problem for a semi-cyclic holey group divisible design of type $(n,m^t)$ with block size $3$, which is denoted by a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(n,m^t)$. When $t$ is odd and $n\neq 8$ or $t$ is doubly even and $t\neq 8$, the existence problem is completely solved; when $t$ is singly even, many infinite families are obtained. Applications of our results to two-dimensional balanced sampling plans and optimal two-dimensional optical orthogonal codes are also discussed.
    46	
    47	\noindent {\bf Keywords}: holey group divisible design; semi-cyclic; two-dimensional balanced sampling plan; two-dimensional optical orthogonal code
    48	
    49	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    50	
    51	\section{Introduction}
    52	
    53	Group divisible designs are one of the most important combinatorial structures, which were widely used in constructing other combinatorial configurations \cite{bjl}. Let $K$ be a set of positive integers. A {\em group divisible
    54	design} (GDD), denoted $K$-GDD, is a triple ($X, {\cal G},{\cal B}$)
    55	satisfying the following properties:
    56	\begin{enumerate}
    57	\item[(1)] $X$ is a finite set of {\em points};
    58	
    59	\item[(2)] $\cal G$ is a partition of $X$ into subsets (called {\em groups});
    60	
    61	\item[(3)] $\cal B$ is a set of subsets (called {\em blocks}) of $X$, each of
    62	cardinality from $K$, such that every $2$-subset of $X$ is either
    63	contained in exactly one block or in exactly one group, but not in
    64	both.
    65	\end{enumerate}
    66	If $\cal G$ contains $u_i$ groups of size $g_i$ for $1\leq
    67	i\leq r$, then we call $g_1^{u_1}g_2^{u_2}\cdots g_r^{u_r}$ the {\em
    68	group type} (or {\em type}) of the GDD. If $K=\{k\}$, we write a
    69	$\{k\}$-GDD as a $k$-GDD. A $K$-$GDD$ of type $1^v$ is commonly called a
    70	{\em pairwise balanced design}, denoted by a $(v,K,1)$-$PBD$. When $K=\{k\}$, a pairwise balanced design is called a {\em balanced incomplete block design}, denoted by a $(v,
    71	k,1)$-BIBD.
    72	
    73	A sub-GDD $(Y,{\cal H},{\cal A})$ of a GDD $(X,{\cal G},{\cal B})$ is a GDD satisfying that $Y\subseteq X$, ${\cal A}\subseteq{\cal B}$, and every group of $\cal H$ is contained in some group of $\cal G$. If a GDD has a missing sub-GDD, then we say that the GDD has a {\em hole}. In fact, the missing sub-GDD need not exist. If a GDD has several equal-sized holes which partition the point set of the GDD, then we call it a holey GDD, or HGDD. We give a formal definition of an HGDD as follows.
    74	
    75	
    76	
    77	Let $n,m$ and $t$ be positive integers. Let $K$ be a set of positive integers. A {\em holey group divisible design} (HGDD) $K$-HGDD of type $(n,m^t)$ is a quadruple $(X,{\cal G},{\cal H},{\cal B})$ which satisfies the following properties:
    78	\begin{enumerate}
    79	    \item[(1)] $X$ is a finite set of $nmt$ {\em points};
    80	    \item[(2)] ${\cal G}$ is a partition of $X$ into $n$ subsets, (called {\em groups}), each of size $mt$;
    81	    \item[(3)] ${\cal H}$ is another partition of $X$ into $t$ subsets, (called {\em holes}), each of size $nm$ such that $|H\cap G|=m$ for each $H\in {\cal H}$ and $G\in {\cal G}$;
    82	    \item[(4)] $\cal B$ is a set of subsets (called {\em blocks}) of $X$, each of
    83	    cardinality from $K$, such that no block contains two distinct points of any group or any hole, but any other pair of distinct points of $X$ occurs in exactly one block of $\cal B$.
    84	\end{enumerate}
    85	When $m=1$, a $K$-HGDD of type $(n,1^t)$ is often said to be a {\em modified group divisible design}, denoted by a $K$-MGDD of type $t^n$. If $K=\{k\}$, we write a $\{k\}$-HGDD as a $k$-HGDD, and a $\{k\}$-MGDD as a $k$-MGDD.
    86	
    87	Assaf \cite{a} first introduced the notion of MGDDs and settled the existence of $3$-MGDDs. The existence of $4$-MGDDs was investigated in \cite{aw,gww,lc}. There are also some results on $5$-MGDDs in \cite{aa}. Wei \cite{wei} first introduced the concept of HGDDs and gave a complete existence theorem for $3$-HGDDs. The existence of $4$-HGDDs has been completely settled in \cite{cww,gw}. We only quote the following result for the later use.
    88	
    89	\begin{Theorem}\label{3-HGDD}{\rm \cite{wei}}
    90	There exists a $3$-HGDD of type $(n,m^t)$ if and only if $n,t\geq 3$, $(t-1)(n-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$ and $t(t-1)n(n-1)m^2\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 3)$.
    91	\end{Theorem}
    92	
    93	HGDDs can be seen as a special case of double group divisible designs (DGDDs), which are introduced in \cite{zhu} to simplify Stinson's proof \cite{s} on a recursive construction for group divisible designs. HGDDs can also be considered as a generalization of holey mutually orthogonal Latin squares (HMOLS), since a $k$-HGDD of type $(k,m^t)$ is equivalent to $k-2$ HMOLSs of type $m^t$ (see \cite{sz,wei} for details). HGDDs have been used for various types of combinatorial objects, such as covering designs and packing designs \cite{a2}, balanced sampling designs \cite{bcrw,gww}, etc.
    94	
    95	In what follows we always assume that $I_u=\{0,1,\ldots,u-1\}$ and denote by $Z_v$ the additive group of integers modulo $v$. A way to construct $k$-HGDDs of type $(n,m^t)$ is the pure and mixed difference method. Let $S=\{0,t,\ldots,(m-1)t\}$ be a subgroup of order $m$ in $Z_{mt}$, and $S_l=S+l$ be a coset of $S$ in $Z_{mt}$, $0\leq l\leq t-1$. Let $X=I_n\times Z_{mt}$, ${\cal G}=\{\{i\}\times Z_{mt}:i\in I_n\}$, and ${\cal H}=\{I_n\times S_l:0\leq l\leq t-1\}$. Take a family ${\cal B}^*$ of some $k$-subsets (called {\em base blocks}) of $X$. For $i,j\in I_n$ and $B\in{\cal B}^*$, define a multi-set $\Delta_{ij}(B)=\{x-y\ ({\rm mod}\ mt): (i,x),(j,y)\in B, (i,x)\neq(j,y)\}$, and a multi-set $\Delta_{ij}({\cal B}^*)=\bigcup_{B\in{\cal B}^*}\Delta_{ij}(B)$. If for $(i,j)\in I_n\times I_n$,
    96	$$\Delta_{ij}({\cal B}^*)=\left\{
    97	\begin{array}{lll}
    98	Z_{mt}\setminus S, & \ \ i\neq j,\\
    99	\emptyset, & \ \ i=j,\\
   100	\end{array}
   101	\right.
   102	$$
   103	then a $k$-HGDD of type $(n,m^t)$ with the point set $X$, the group set $\cal G$ and the hole set $\cal H$ can be generated from ${\cal B}^*$. The required blocks are
   104	obtained by developing all base blocks of ${\cal B}^*$ by successively
   105	adding $1$ to the second component of each point of these base
   106	blocks modulo $mt$. Usually a $k$-HGDD obtained by this
   107	manner is said to be a {\em semi-cyclic $k$-HGDD} and denoted by a $k$-SCHGDD.
   108	
   109	In this paper we shall focus on the existence of $3$-SCHGDDs of type $(n,m^t)$ with $t$ even. When $t$ is odd, the existence problem has been discussed in \cite{fwc} recently.
   110	
   111	\begin{Lemma}\label{odd main results}{\rm \cite{fwc}}
   112	\begin{enumerate}
   113	\item[$(1)$] There exists a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(3,m^t)$ if and only if $(t-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$ and $t\geq 3$ with the exception of $m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$ and $t=3$.
   114	\item[$(2)$] Assume that $t\equiv 1\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$ and $n\geq 4$. There exists a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(n,m^t)$ if and only if $t\geq 3$ and $(t-1)n(n-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 6)$ except when $(n,m,t)=(6,1,3)$, and possibly when $(1)$ $n=6$, $m\equiv 1,5\ ({\rm mod}\ 6)$ and $t\equiv 3,15\ ({\rm mod}\ 18)$, $(2)$ $n=8$, $m\equiv 2,10\ ({\rm mod}\ 12)$ and $t\equiv 7\ ({\rm mod}\ 12)$.
   115	\end{enumerate}
   116	\end{Lemma}
   117	
   118	Simple counting shows that the number of base blocks in a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(n,m^t)$ is $(t-1)n(n-1)m/6$. Hence combining the result of Theorem \ref{3-HGDD}, we have the following necessary condition for the existence of $3$-SCHGDDs.
   119	
   120	\begin{Lemma}\label{nece}
   121	If there exists a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(n,m^t)$, then $n,t\geq 3$, $(t-1)(n-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$ and $(t-1)n(n-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 6)$.
   122	\end{Lemma}
   123	
   124	We are to prove in Sections $3$ and $4$ that when $t$ is odd and $n\neq 8$ or $t$ is doubly even and $t\neq 8$, the existence problem for $3$-SCHGDDs is completely solved, and when $t$ is singly even, many infinite families are obtained.
   125	
   126	Although the existence problem for SCHGDDs is interesting in its own right, there are actually some nice applications such as in the construction of two-dimensional balanced sampling plans and optimal two-dimensional optical orthogonal codes. We shall consider these applications in Section $5$. Actually, the  main result of this paper has been used for constructing optimal three dimensional optical orthogonal codes by Wang and Chang \cite{wlc}.
   127	
   128	%To conclude an application of SCHGDDs to some interesting sampling designs are mentioned in Section $5$.
   129	
   130	\section{Preliminaries}
   131	
   132	Now we introduce some basic concepts and terminologies in
   133	combinatorial design theory adopted in this paper.
   134	
   135	An {\em automorphism} $\pi$ of a GDD $(X,{\cal G},{\cal B})$ is a permutation on $X$ leaving ${\cal G}$, ${\cal B}$ invariant, respectively. Let $H$ be the cyclic group
   136	generated by $\pi$ under the compositions of permutations. Then all blocks of the GDD can be partitioned into some block orbits under $H$. Choose any fixed block from each block orbit and then call it a {\em base block} of this GDD under $H$. The number of the blocks contained in a block orbit is called the {\em length} of the block orbit.
   137	
   138	A $K$-GDD of type $m^n$ is said to be {\em cyclic}, if it admits an automorphism consisting of a cycle of length $mn$. A cyclic $K$-GDD is denoted by a $K$-CGDD. For a $K$-CGDD $(X,{\cal G},{\cal B})$, we can always identify $X$ with $Z_{mn}$ and ${\cal G}$ with $\{\{in+j : 0\leq i\leq m-1\} : 0\leq j\leq n-1\}$. If the length of each block orbit in a $K$-CGDD of type $m^n$ is $mn$, then the $K$-GDD is called {\em strictly cyclic}.
   139	
   140	\begin{Lemma}\label{3-sCGDD}{\rm \cite{wc}}
   141	There exists a strictly cyclic $3$-GDD of type $m^n$ if and only if
   142	\begin{enumerate}
   143	\item[$(1)$] $m(n-1)\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 6)$ and $n\geq 4;$
   144	\item[$(2)$] $n\not\equiv 2,3\ ({\rm mod}\ 4)$ when $m\equiv 2\ ({\rm mod}\ 4).$
   145	\end{enumerate}
   146	\end{Lemma}
   147	
   148	\begin{Construction} \label{SCHGDD-from strictly CGDD}{\rm \cite{fwc}}
   149	Suppose that there exist a strictly cyclic $K$-GDD of type $w^t$ and an $l$-MGDD of type $k^n$ for each $k\in K$. Then there exists an $l$-SCHGDD of type $(n,w^t)$.
   150	\end{Construction}
   151	
   152	Construction \ref{SCHGDD-from strictly CGDD} shows that strictly cyclic $K$-GDDs are helpful to yield SCHGDDs. Combining the results of Corollary $4.10$ and Lemma $4.15$ in \cite{fwc}, we have the following strictly cyclic GDDs.
   153	
   154	\begin{Lemma}\label{4^t-0,1,2mod3} {\rm \cite{fwc}}
   155	There exists a strictly cyclic $\{3,5\}$-GDD of type $4^t$ for any $t\geq 4$ and $t\not\in\{5,8,11\}$.
   156	\end{Lemma}
   157	
   158	Let $K$ be a set of positive integers and $v$ be an odd positive integer. For each $1\leq i\leq b$, let $B_i\subseteq Z_v$ and $|B_i|=k_i\in K$. The $b$ sets $B_i=\{x_{i,1}, x_{i,2},\ldots,x_{i,k}\}$, $1\leq i\leq b$, form a {\em perfect $(v,K, 1)$ difference family}, written as a $(v,K,1)$-PDF, if all the differences $x_{i,s}-x_{i,r}$, $1\leq i\leq b$, $1\leq r < s \leq k_i$ cover exactly the set $\{1,2,\ldots,(v-1)/2\}$.
   159	
   160	\begin{Lemma}\label{relation PDF and GDD} {\rm \cite{fwc}} If there exists a $(2t-1,K,1)$-PDF, then there exists a strictly cyclic $K$-GDD of type $2^t$.
   161	\end{Lemma}
   162	
   163	\begin{Lemma}
   164	{\rm \cite{czl} }\label{PDF-24-19}
   165	There exists a $(v,\{3,4\},1)$-PDF for $v\equiv 1\ ({\rm mod}\ 6)$ and $v\geq 19$.
   166	\end{Lemma}
   167	
   168	A $K$-GDD of type $m^n$ is said to be {\em semi-cyclic}, if it admits an automorphism which permutes the elements of each group $G\in{\cal G}$ in an $m$ cycle. Such a GDD is denoted by a $K$-SCGDD of type $m^n$. For a $K$-SCGDD $(X,{\cal G},{\cal B})$, we can always identify $X$ with $I_n\times Z_m$ and ${\cal G}$ with $\{\{i\}\times Z_m:i\in I_n\}$. In this case the automorphism can be taken as $(i,x)\longmapsto(i,x+1)$ (mod $(-,m)$), $i\in I_n$ and $x\in Z_m$. Assume that ${\cal B}^*$ is the set of base blocks of a $K$-SCGDD of type $m^n$. It is easy to verify that for $(i,j)\in I_n\times I_n$,
   169	$$\Delta_{ij}({\cal B}^*)=\left\{
   170	\begin{array}{lll}
   171	Z_m, & \ \ \ i\neq j,\\
   172	\emptyset, & \ \ \ i=j.\\
   173	\end{array}
   174	\right.
   175	$$
   176	Note that no element of $Z_m$ occurs more than once in $\Delta_{ij}({\cal B}^*)$, and the length of each block orbit is $m$ in a $K$-SCGDD.
   177	
   178	\begin{Lemma}{\rm\cite{jiang}}\label{scgdd}
   179	There is a $3$-SCGDD of type $m^n$ if and only if $n\geq 3$ and
   180	\begin{enumerate}
   181	\item[$(1)$] $(n-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$;
   182	\item[$(2)$] $n(n-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 3)$;
   183	\item[$(3)$] $n\not\equiv 2,3\ ({\rm mod}\ 4)$ when $m\equiv 2\ ({\rm mod}\ 4)$, and $n\neq 3$ when $m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 4)$.
   184	\end{enumerate}
   185	\end{Lemma}
   186	
   187	\begin{Construction} \label{SCHGDD-recur}{\rm \cite{fwc}}
   188	Suppose that there exist a $K$-SCGDD of type $g^n$ and an $l$-SCHGDD of type $(k,w^t)$ for each $k\in K$. Then there exists an $l$-SCHGDD of type $(n,(gw)^t)$.
   189	\end{Construction}
   190	
   191	A $(k,m)$-CDM is a $k\times m$ matrix $D=(d_{ij})$ with entries from $Z_m$ such that for any two distinct rows $x$ and $y$, the difference list $\{d_{xj}-d_{yj}:j\in I_m\}$ contains each integer of $Z_m$ exactly once. It is  known that a $(3,m)$-CDM exists for any positive odd integer $m$ \cite{bjl}.
   192	
   193	\begin{Construction}\label{SCHGDD from CDM}{\rm \cite{fwc}}
   194	If there exist a $k$-SCHGDD of type
   195	$(n,w^t)$ and a $(k,v)$-CDM, then there exists a $k$-SCHGDD of type $(n,(wv)^t)$.
   196	\end{Construction}
   197	
   198	The following construction is simple but useful.
   199	
   200	\begin{Construction}\label{SCHGDD from SCHGDD}{\rm \cite{fwc}}
   201	If there exist a $k$-SCHGDD of type $(n,(gw)^t)$ and a $k$-SCHGDD of type
   202	$(n,g^w)$, then there exists a $k$-SCHGDD of type $(n,g^{wt})$.
   203	\end{Construction}
   204	
   205	As the end of this section, we state a shorter definition of SCHGDDs. An {\em automorphism} of an HGDD $(X,{\cal G},{\cal H},{\cal B})$ is a permutation on $X$ leaving ${\cal G}$, ${\cal H}$, $\cal B$ invariant, respectively. A $K$-SCHGDD of type
   206	$(n,m^t)$ is a $K$-HGDD of type $(n,m^t)$
   207	with an automorphism of order $mt$ that permutes the points within each group.
   208	
   209	
   210	\section{$3$-SCHGDDs of type $(n,m^t)$ for $n=4,5,6,8$}
   211	
   212	In this section, we always assume that $[a,b]$ denotes the set of integers $n$ such that $a\leq n\leq b$, and $[a,b]_{\not\equiv r(v)}$, $0\leq r\leq v-1$, denotes the set of integers $n$ such that $a\leq n\leq b$ and $n\not\equiv r\ ({\rm mod}\ v)$.
   213	
   214	\begin{Lemma}\label{4-SCHGDD}
   215	If $(t-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$ and $t\geq 3$, then there exists a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(4,m^t)$.
   216	\end{Lemma}
   217	
   218	\proof When $t$ is odd, the conclusion follows from Lemma \ref{odd main results}. When $m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 4)$, start from a $3$-SCGDD of type $(m/2)^4$, which exists by Lemma \ref{scgdd}. By Lemma \ref{odd main results}, there exists a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(3,2^t)$ for any even integer $t\geq 4$. Then apply Construction \ref{SCHGDD-recur} to obtain a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(4,m^t)$.
