   561	\begin{Lemma}\label{6-SCHGDD}
   562	If $(t-1)m\equiv 0\ ({\rm mod}\ 2)$ and $t\geq 3$, then there exists a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(6,m^t)$ with the exception of $(m,t)=(1,3)$.
   563	\end{Lemma}
   564	
   565	\proof When $t$ is odd with the exception of $m\equiv 1,5\ ({\rm mod}\ 6)$ and $t\equiv 3,15\ ({\rm mod}\ 18)$, the conclusion follows from Lemma \ref{odd main results}. When $m=1$ and $t=3$, by Lemma \ref{odd main results}, there is no $3$-SCHGDD of type $(6,1^3)$. When $m=1$, $t\equiv 3,15\ ({\rm mod}\ 18)$ and $t\geq 15$, start from a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(6,(t/3)^3)$, which exists by Lemma \ref{6-SCHGDD-m^3}. Then apply Construction \ref{SCHGDD from SCHGDD} with a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(6,1^{t/3})$ to obtain a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(6,1^t)$.
   566	
   567	When $m\equiv 1,5\ ({\rm mod}\ 6)$ and $t\equiv 3,15\ ({\rm mod}\ 18)$, start from a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(6,1^t)$, and apply Construction \ref{SCHGDD from CDM} with a $(3,m)$-CDM to obtain a $3$-SCHGDD of type $(6,m^t)$.
